<p>Ukraine marked four years since Russia launched its full-scale invasion, holding remembrance events and unveiling memorials as the war moved into a fifth year and fighting continued. President Volodymyr Zelensky urged national unity and cast Ukraine’s continued resistance as the country’s defining answer to the invasion. Several outlets described a grinding, costly conflict with no clear military winner so far, increasingly shaped by drone warfare and persistent strikes along the front. European leaders traveled to Kyiv to honor the dead and reaffirm support, even as commentary painted that backing as either steady or strained by disagreements within Europe.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Mi8gbSQu__rRLA2KLVEnb-_RtXi0Gzvwtolo45J0UI7sIAV423AAVgD56_wCnuNn5dvdQW2l77o0uD1H1knunuX9xmDhO4HCgSHmwLVY3oM6_DCXuFhxndyH77-DfBWCt2p_VP4dadM8sGsW' alt='Illustrative image used with PBS report on the war’s toll entering its fifth year.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Drone battlefield: Front-line reporting described near-constant drone activity above Ukraine’s “snaking” contact lines, with drones used for long-range strikes and for close-in surveillance and targeting near soldiers.</li><li>No end in sight: PBS reported little sign the war will end soon, as Ukrainians keep navigating the pressure on daily life and mounting losses.</li><li>Kyiv region vigils: Residents in places including Irpin and Bucha lit candles and held memorial observances, even as battles continued in the east, according to Euronews video reporting.</li><li>Zelensky’s outlook: Semafor reported Zelensky told the Financial Times the war is at the “beginning of the end” as the conflict entered its fifth year.</li><li>Life stories: Meduza published letters from readers in Ukraine and Russia describing how air-raid alerts, fear for family members, and emotional strain have reshaped daily life over the past four years.</li></ul><blockquote>Our people did not raise a white flag — they defended the blue 